{{nihongo|'''Kentaro Tokuyama'''|徳山 健太郎|Tokuyama Kentarō}} (27 April 1923 – 18 January 2020) was a Sanese politician who served as the 39th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from August 1989 to October 1990. A member of the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP), his brief tenure was consumed by the [[Miyazaki hotel scandal]], which exposed systemic corruption linking LDP leaders, construction magnates, and organized crime. Tokuyama resigned in disgrace, becoming the first post-war prime minister to leave office under the shadow of a major corruption scandal.

== Early life ==
Tokuyama was born in [[Shimabara Prefecture]] in 1923, the son of a local government official. His family had a long tradition of public service, and Tokuyama was expected to follow his father into the bureaucracy. He attended [[Okami University]], where he studied law and developed an interest in local governance and regional development.

After graduating in 1945, Tokuyama entered the Home Ministry, where he served in several prefectural offices before transitioning to electoral politics. His early career was marked by a reputation for diligence and organizational skill, qualities that would serve him well in the faction-ridden world of LDP politics.

== Political career ==
Tokuyama was first elected to the House of Commoners in 1958, representing [[Shimabara 4th District]]. He built his political base through the traditional LDP method of constituency service and factional loyalty, rising through the party ranks as a reliable lieutenant of the dominant Kato faction.

During the [[Go-go 8090]] era, Tokuyama served as Minister of Construction, where he oversaw the massive public works programs that were both the engine of the economic boom and the primary vehicle for the patronage networks that sustained the LDP's political dominance. His tenure was characterized by an aggressive expansion of infrastructure spending, including the construction of highways, bridges, and public buildings that were widely criticized as unnecessary but that generated enormous profits for the construction companies that were the LDP's most reliable financial supporters.

Tokuyama's close ties to the construction industry made him a natural candidate for the premiership when the Kato administration ended in 1989. He was selected as LDP president and prime minister as a compromise candidate acceptable to all major factions—a decision that would prove disastrous when the Miyazaki hotel scandal erupted.

== Premiership (1989–1990) ==
=== The Miyazaki hotel scandal ===
[[File:Miyazaki Hotel Scandal Press Conference 1990.png|thumb|right|200px|Press conference following the revelation of the Miyazaki hotel scandal in early 1990]]
The [[Miyazaki hotel scandal]] broke in early 1990, when investigative journalists revealed that a network of LDP politicians, construction magnates, and organized crime figures had been using the Miyazaki Hotel—a luxury resort in southern Okaiken—as a front for bribery, money laundering, and illegal political donations. The scandal implicated senior figures across the LDP, including several cabinet ministers and faction leaders.

Tokuyama's personal involvement in the scandal was a matter of intense debate. While he was never directly charged with any crime, his long association with the construction industry and his role as Minister of Construction during the period when the illegal activities were occurring made it impossible for him to claim ignorance. Revelations that his office had accepted large donations from companies implicated in the scandal eroded whatever public credibility he retained.

The scandal's impact was devastating. Public trust in the LDP collapsed, with approval ratings for the party falling below 20% for the first time in its history. Mass protests in Okami and other major cities demanded the government's resignation, and opposition parties seized the opportunity to attack the LDP's culture of corruption.

=== Resignation ===
Tokuyama initially attempted to weather the storm, reshuffling his cabinet and promising a thorough investigation. However, when it became clear that the scandal reached into the highest levels of the party and that his own position was untenable, he announced his resignation on 7 October 1990. He was succeeded by [[Hideaki Fujio]], who was tasked with salvaging the party's reputation.

In his resignation statement, Tokuyama acknowledged that "the trust placed in me by the people has been broken" and accepted "full responsibility for the failures of governance that have been revealed." He retired from active politics shortly thereafter, though he retained his Diet seat until 1996.

=== Domestic policy ===
Despite the scandal that consumed his premiership, the Tokuyama government pursued several significant domestic policy initiatives before the crisis erupted. The government introduced the Regional Revitalization Act of 1989, which provided funding for economic development projects in rural areas that had been left behind by the Go-go 8090 economic boom. The Act was a response to the growing problem of rural depopulation, as young people continued to migrate to the major cities in search of economic opportunity.

The government also introduced the Financial Institutions Reform Act of 1989, which tightened regulations on the banking sector in response to the growing problem of non-performing loans. The Act was a modest attempt to address the structural weaknesses in the financial system that would later contribute to the Lost Decade, but it was widely criticized as insufficient.

On the social policy front, the Tokuyama government introduced the Elder Care Insurance Act of 1990, which established a national long-term care insurance program for the elderly. The Act was a response to the nation's rapidly aging population and the growing burden on families caring for elderly relatives. The program was funded through a combination of payroll taxes and government subsidies, and it represented a significant expansion of the social safety net.

=== Foreign policy ===
The Tokuyama government's foreign policy was largely overshadowed by the domestic political crisis, but several significant developments occurred during his brief tenure. The government continued Okaiken's active participation in the [[Adonian Community]], supporting the organization's efforts to deepen economic integration among its member states. The government also negotiated the Adonian Community Trade Facilitation Agreement of 1989, which reduced non-tariff barriers to trade among member states.

In the Shansian relationship, the Tokuyama government pursued a cautious approach, maintaining the existing diplomatic framework while avoiding the territorial disputes that had periodically disrupted bilateral relations. The government authorized the renewal of the Shansian-Sanese Fisheries Agreement, which provided for the cooperative management of fishing rights in the disputed northern waters.

The government also sought to strengthen Okaiken's relationship with the Shendan states, dispatching trade missions to several Shendan nations and negotiating bilateral investment agreements. The Shendan Economic Partnership Initiative of 1989, which provided for increased Sanese investment in Shendan infrastructure and natural resource development, was the government's most significant foreign policy initiative.

== Legacy ==
Tokuyama's premiership is remembered as the moment when the LDP's decades-long dominance of Sanese politics began to unravel. The Miyazaki hotel scandal exposed the systemic corruption that had been an open secret in Sanese politics for years, and it destroyed the public's willingness to tolerate the party's patronage-driven governance model.

The scandal's consequences extended far beyond Tokuyama's resignation. It triggered a political crisis that would see four prime ministers in rapid succession, the collapse of the LDP's governing coalition, and the eventual emergence of the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]] as a credible alternative to the LDP's long rule. In this sense, Tokuyama's downfall was the beginning of the end of the post-war political order.

Tokuyama himself lived in quiet retirement until his death in 2020 at the age of 96. He never publicly discussed the scandal in detail, and his memoirs—published in 2005—contained only a brief and defensive account of his premiership.

